cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  ScalingElections: Condorcet Voting at GPU Speed —
  Schulze as semiring matrix multiplication, Kemeny-Young as exact NP-hard search
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.2.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22073377
repository-code: https://github.com/ashvardanian/ScalingElections
url: https://ashvardanian.com/posts/scaling-elections/
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22073378
    description: "v0.2.0 — version DOI"
abstract: >-
  Voting at scale looks like a graph problem and computes like a dense one. The Schulze method closes
  the pairwise tally under a max-min recurrence, and that recurrence is matrix multiplication with
  addition replaced by maximum and multiplication by minimum — a product over the (max, min) semiring
  rather than over the reals. Written that way, the whole tiled-GEMM playbook transfers unchanged:
  blocking into tiles, staging them through shared memory and through the bulk-tensor engine on
  Hopper, and a three-phase schedule in which only the tiles straddling the diagonal owe a barrier
  while every other update degenerates to a plain maximum. The answer does not depend on that
  reordering, only the speed does, so the exact winner arrives at throughputs usually reserved for
  sampling. Split Cycle rides the identical kernel and differs only in the edge weights fed to it,
  winning votes for Schulze and positive margins for Split Cycle.

  Kemeny-Young admits no such semiring. Its consensus ranking is NP-hard, and it is solved here
  exactly rather than approximately, by dynamic programming over candidate subsets whose table is
  split into low and high halves so it stays cache-resident while the score table streams past. That
  bounds it to small fields, which is the honest price of exactness and the reason both machines
  share one repository: the cubic methods run to tens of thousands of candidates, the exponential one
  cannot, and the boundary between them is the interesting part. Kernels cover NVIDIA GPUs through
  CUDA C++, AMD GPUs through ROCm/HIP, and CPUs through OpenMP and Arm NEON, alongside a Mojo
  implementation spanning CPU, SIMD, and GPU paths, all validated against serial NumPy and Numba
  references.
